# Answer 8 · Principles, Goals and Strategies of the Waves, and Their Effect on Women's Lives (CSS 2025)
@py question 2025-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Treat every wave under the same three sub-headings — principles, goals, strategies — then give the effects on women's lives area by area, with Pakistan and the unfinished agenda.
@ask ① DISCUSS the key PRINCIPLES, GOALS and STRATEGIES of the different WAVES
@ask ② HOW have these waves AFFECTED WOMEN'S LIVES?
(1) Introduction: waves are bursts of organised activity; four waves; each widened the meaning of equality
(2) First wave: women are rational citizens; vote, property, education; petitions and militancy
(3) Second wave: the personal is political; pay, body and violence; lawsuits and consciousness-raising
(4) Third wave: diversity and intersectionality; inclusion and identity; culture and networks
(5) Fourth wave: consent and accountability; ending harassment; hashtags and mass marches
(6) Effects on political and legal life: votes, offices, property and non-discrimination laws
(7) Effects on education, work and health: universities, professions, pay laws, reproductive rights
(8) Effects on safety and voice: violence criminalised; diverse women heard; harassment costly
(9) Effects in Pakistan: vote in 1947; laws from 1961 to 2025; Aurat March
(10) Unfinished agenda: 120 years to parity; violence; reversals; Pakistan 143rd of 145
(11) Conclusion: one long movement that made women citizens, workers, owners of their bodies and voices
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Discuss the key principles, goals and strategies"** sets three fixed sub-headings for **each** wave — use them every time, so the examiner can tick them off. "**Different** waves" allows all four; include the **fourth**. **"How have these waves affected women's lives?"** asks for **effects**, best organised by **area of life** (politics, law, education, work, health, safety, voice), with evidence, a Pakistan angle and an honest note on what is unfinished.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
A **wave** of feminism is a period of intense, organised activity for women's rights with shared principles, goals and methods. The journalist **Martha Weinman Lear** made the metaphor popular in ==1968==.

#### ii) The four waves
The **first** (c. ==1848==–==1920==), **second** (==1960s==–==1980s==), **third** (==1990s==–==2000s==) and **fourth** (from about ==2012==).

#### iii) The argument
Each wave **widened the meaning of equality** — from the law, to private life, to differences among women, to everyday safety — and each changed women's lives in lasting ways, though the work is unfinished.

### 2. The first wave (c. 1848–1920)

#### i) Principles
Women are **rational human beings** with the same natural rights as men (**Wollstonecraft**, ==1792==; **J. S. Mill**, ==1869==). This is **liberal** feminism: equality before the law.

#### ii) Goals
The **vote**; **property rights** for married women, ending **coverture**; **education** and entry to the **professions**; custody of children and fairer divorce.

#### iii) Strategies
The **Seneca Falls Convention** (==1848==) and its **Declaration of Sentiments**; petitions and lobbying by **suffragists** (NUWSS, ==1897==); **militant** action by **suffragettes** (WSPU, ==1903==) — window-smashing and hunger strikes under the slogan "Deeds, not words". Newspapers, public meetings and huge processions kept the demand in the public eye for seventy years.

### 3. The second wave (1960s–1980s)

#### i) Principles
Legal equality is not enough; inequality lives in **work, family, sexuality and culture**. "**The personal is political**" (**Carol Hanisch**, ==1970==).

#### ii) Goals
**Equal pay** and access to jobs; **reproductive rights**; an end to **domestic violence, rape and sexual harassment**; shared housework and childcare.

#### iii) Strategies
**Lobbying and lawsuits** by the liberal stream (**NOW**, ==1966==, **Betty Friedan**); **consciousness-raising groups**, marches and protests by the radical stream; building **shelters** (London, ==1971==) and rape crisis centres; founding **Women's Studies**. Paperback books such as *The Feminine Mystique* (==1963==) and magazines such as *Ms.* (==1972==) carried the ideas to millions of women at home.

### 4. The third wave (1990s–2000s)

#### i) Principles
Women are **diverse**; gender **intersects** with race, class and sexuality (**Crenshaw**, ==1989==); gender is **constructed** (Butler, ==1990==); each woman may define feminism for herself.

#### ii) Goals
**Inclusion** of women of colour, lesbian and transgender women and women of the global South; sexual freedom and positive self-image; action on **harassment and violence**; global **human rights**.

#### iii) Strategies
**Culture** — Riot Grrrl music, **zines**, art and media; voter registration (**Rebecca Walker**'s Third Wave organisation, ==1992==); transnational networks at **Beijing** (==1995==). The early internet and email let activists in different continents share information quickly for the first time.

### 5. The fourth wave (c. 2012 onward)

#### i) Principles
**Consent**, **bodily autonomy**, **accountability** of the powerful, and intersectionality as a starting point.

#### ii) Goals
Ending **sexual harassment and violence** at work, in public and online; safe spaces; equal pay.

#### iii) Strategies
**Hashtag campaigns** (#MeToo, ==2017==; #NiUnaMenos, ==2015==), online **testimony**, viral videos and mass marches organised through social media — such as the **Women's March** of ==21 January 2017==. Campaigns now spread across countries within days.

::: fig pyramid | How each wave deepened the meaning of equality.
First wave: equality before the law — women as citizens
Second wave: equality in work, family and body
Third wave: equality among women — diversity
Fourth wave: equality in everyday life — safety and consent
:::

### 6. Effects on political and legal life

#### i) Votes and office
Women now vote almost everywhere; **New Zealand** led in ==1893==. By the mid-2020s about a quarter of the world's parliamentarians were women, and many countries had elected women heads of government.

#### ii) Legal personhood
The age of coverture is over: married women own property, sign contracts, keep their wages and seek divorce; discrimination on the basis of sex is unlawful in most countries, and **CEDAW** (==1979==) sets a global standard.

### 7. Effects on education, work and health

#### i) Education and work
Women outnumber men at university in many countries and work in every profession, helped by laws such as the US **Equal Pay Act** (==1963==) and **Title VII** (==1964==). Pay gaps and "glass ceilings" remain, and women still do most unpaid care work everywhere — the "second shift" after paid work.

#### ii) Health and the body
The contraceptive pill and legal abortion in many countries (***Roe v. Wade***, ==1973==) gave women control over childbearing — though ***Dobbs*** (==2022==) shows such gains can be reversed.

### 8. Effects on safety and voice

#### i) Safety
Rape, domestic violence and harassment became **crimes**; shelters and helplines exist; **#MeToo** made harassment costly for powerful men. Marital rape, once legal almost everywhere, is now a crime in many countries, and workplaces have complaint procedures.

#### ii) Voice
Women of colour, lesbian and transgender women and women of the South speak for themselves; ordinary women share experiences with millions online. The idea that women's experience is valid knowledge — a second-wave principle — has become common sense.

### 9. Effects in Pakistan

#### i) Law and politics
Pakistani women received the **vote in 1947**. Family, criminal and political reforms followed: the **MFLO** (==1961==), the **Protection of Women Act** (==2006==), laws on **harassment** (==2010==) and **honour killing** (==2016==), **60** reserved seats (==2002==) and the **Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act** (==2025==).

#### ii) Voice
The **Aurat March** (from ==2018==) brought fourth-wave activism to Pakistani streets.

#### iii) Limits
The waves reached Pakistan **out of order and together**: first-wave goals such as girls' schooling and inheritance are unfinished while fourth-wave campaigns grow online. Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report ==2026==, and only ==24.4%== of women are in the labour force.

### 10. The unfinished agenda
The world had closed only ==69.2%== of its gender gap in ==2026==, with parity ==120 years== away. About **one in three** women worldwide has experienced physical or sexual violence (WHO, ==2021==). Gains can be reversed, as the end of *Roe v. Wade* and restrictions on women in Afghanistan show. The **Beijing+30** review (==2025==) warned of a growing backlash against women's rights in many countries.

### 11. Conclusion
The waves differ in principles, goals and strategies, but they form **one long movement**. The first made women **citizens**; the second gave them rights at **work and over their bodies**; the third made feminism **plural**; the fourth made **harassment visible**. Women's lives have been transformed — but in countries like Pakistan the transformation is still partial. The next wave's task is to carry these gains to the rural, poor and marginalised women whom earlier waves reached last.

## Why this answer scores
- **Principles, goals and strategies** appear as fixed sub-headings for every wave — exactly what the question asks.
- The **fourth wave** is included, answering "different waves" in full.
- **Effects** are organised by **area of life**, each with evidence, plus Pakistan and the unfinished agenda.
- A **pyramid figure** summarises how equality deepened.
